function statusTable(){const rows=CATNAMES.map((n,i)=>{const L=CATALOG.filter(e=>e.c===i),c=k=>L.filter(e=>e.k===k).length;return[n,c('dedicated'),c('nearest'),c('supplier')]});const T=k=>CATALOG.filter(e=>e.k===k).length;
 return`<table class="st"><tr><th>Category</th><th>Dedic.</th><th>Near.</th><th>Suppl.</th></tr>${rows.map(r=>`<tr><td>${r[0]}</td>${r.slice(1).map(x=>`<td>${x}</td>`).join('')}</tr>`).join('')}<tr class="tot"><td>Total 1,410</td><td>${T('dedicated')}</td><td>${T('nearest')}</td><td>${T('supplier')}</td></tr></table><p class="note">All 1,410 items open with Dieline, Mockup, Spec board, Scene and Board.</p>`}
function docsHtml(){return`<div class="card wide"><h2>What this tool is</h2><p>A parametric production tool: every template is computed from your numbers, then exported as editable vectors at 1:1. 1,410 catalogue items are mapped onto ${GENS.length} verified generator families plus mockups and boards. Nothing is marked Available unless its generator exists and its output was checked.</p>
<h2>Template types</h2><p>Every item is active with all five views. <b>Dedicated template</b> — the item has its own parametric generator. <b>Nearest template</b> — the item uses the closest working family (for example a pencil holder uses the tray); adjust the dimensions to the real object. <b>Supplier shape</b> — moulded or manufactured objects (bottles, tubes, tins): the template gives the printable artwork area; match it to the supplier’s blank before production. Structural boxes are geometry-checked; a signed physical sample is still the production approval.</p>
<h2>Generators</h2><table><tr><th>Family</th><th>Type</th><th>Status</th><th>Items using it</th></tr>${GENS.map(g=>`<tr><td>${g.n}</td><td>${g.t}</td><td>${STL[g.st][0]}</td><td>${CATALOG.filter(e=>e.g===g.g).length}</td></tr>`).join('')}</table>
<h2>Mockups & boards</h2><p>${Object.keys(PM).length-1} product mockups are generated from the template dimensions, so a box mockup is always the same size as its dieline. ${Object.keys(MK).length} scene illustrations cover devices, apparel, vehicles, outdoor and interior applications. ${BOARD_ORDER.length} presentation boards follow the five layouts of the brief, all drawn to scale and exported as editable SVG with live text.</p><h2>Exports</h2><p>PDF 1.4 and EPS with Separation colour spaces named Dieline-Cut, Dieline-Crease, Dieline-Perf, Dieline-Glue, Dieline-Bleed, Dieline-Safe (overprint on), crop marks on Registration. SVG keeps each layer as a named group. DXF R12 with CUT, CREASE, PERF, GLUE layers in mm. Native .ai is produced by the included Illustrator script (Save As .ai) because writing the .ai format directly from a browser is not reliable.</p>
<h2>Add a new item type</h2><pre><code>IT.myBox = {
  name:'My box', sub:'Short description', kind:'A',   // A dieline · B flat · C wrap
  params:[['L','Length','n',100], ['W','Width','n',60], ['style','Style','s','a',['a','b']]],
  presets:{'Small':{L:80,W:50}},
  note:'Shown to the user', doc:'Formula used',
  gen(v){ const s=S(); s.cut.push(P(rect(0,0,v.L,v.W))); s.crease.push(P(ln(v.L/2,0,v.L/2,v.W),false)); return s; },
  check(v,s,W){ if(v.W>v.L) W.push(['warn','Wider than long']); }
};</code></pre><p>Then point catalogue items at it in catalog.js (<code>g:'myBox', p:{L:120}</code>). The library, validation, every export format and the spec board pick it up with no other change.</p>
<h2>Roadmap</h2><p>Next generators: auto-lock (crash-lock) bottom carton, snap-lock bottom, seal-end carton, gable, hexagonal and pyramid boxes, burger clamshell, cup carrier, three-pocket folder, envelope liner, drawer box. Then: 3D fold preview from the same geometry, axonometric environmental scenes with numbered items, multi-board presentation export.</p></div>`}

